es  found that seven out of ten students  said they would get rid of their social  networking accounts if it were not for  fear of being left out of the loop out  of curiosity how many people here have  or have considered deactivating your  social  wow that's almost everyone  that fomo you feel the highlight reels  the social currency  those are all results of a relatively  quote-unquote normal social media  experience  but what if going on social every day  was a terrifying experience where you  not just questioned your self-worth but  you questioned your safety  perhaps the worst stressor on social  media is number four online harassment  40 percent of online adults have  experienced online harassment  73 percent have witnessed it  and the unfortunate reality is that it  is much worse and much more likely if  you're a woman lgbtq person of color  muslim i think you get the point  the problem is that in the news we're  seeing these big stories  we're seeing the 18 year old tyler  clemente who took his life after his  roommate secretly filmed him kissing  another guy and outed him on twitter  we see women like anita sarkeesian being  close to shamed off the internet and  sent death and rape threats for sharing  their feminism we see these stories once  it's too late  but what about the everyday online  harassment what about that ugly snapchat  you sent your friend with the intention  of it being private and now it's up on  facebook and so it's just one photo it's  funny it's just one mean comment it's  not a big deal  but when these micro moments happen over  and over again over time  that's when we have a macro problem  we have to recognize these everyday  instances as well because if they go  unchecked and the effects unnoticed we  are going to have many more tyler  clementi's  the effects aren't always easy to  recognize how many of you have noticed  the notifications at the top of my  screen  yeah and how many of you like me are  bothered that they're not checked  yeah okay here let me check them for you  okay  just one small example of what this can  do to you maybe you simply cannot focus  because your notifications are going off  the handle and you need to check  that need  eventually becomes addiction  regarding social media we are already  experiencing impairments similar to  substance dependencies with every like  you get a shot of that feel-good  chemical dopamine  you gain more of that social currency so  what do we do to feel good we check  likes just one more time  we post  just one more time  we're anxious  if we don't have access  does not sound like every drug you've  ever heard of  yeah  so when that grows when your social  media use goes unconfronted over time  that's when we see the rising levels of  anxiety and depression the fomo the  distractions the highlight reels the  comparisons it's a lot and it's all the  time  the canadian association of mental  health found that as young as grades 7  to 12 students who spent 2 hours a day  on social media reported higher levels  of anxiety depression and suicidal  thoughts  for those of you doing the math that's  as young as 12 years old  now here's the thing  i like social media i do i love it  and hearing what i've said today might  make you think i want you to get off of  it but i don't you see i don't think  it's going anywhere so i'm not going to  waste my time telling you to spend less  time on social media frankly i don't  think abstinence is an option anymore  but that doesn't mean you can't practice  safe social  you see everything i've talked about  today has nothing  and everything to do with social media  what do i mean i mean social media is  neither good nor bad it's just the most  recent tool we're using to do we've  always done tell stories and communicate  with each other  you wouldn't blame samsung television  for a bad tv show  twitter doesn't make people write  hateful posts  so when we talk about this dark side of  social media what we're really talking  about is the dark side of people that  dark side that makes harassers harass  that insecurity that makes you take down  a photo you were excited to share that  dark side that looks at a picture of a  happy family and wonders why yours  doesn't look like that  so as parents as educators as friends as  bosses this dark side is what we need to  focus on  we need preventative strategies and  coping strategies so that when you have  your low days because you will when  you're questioning your self-worth you  never get as low as tyler clementi  then the many others like him  okay bailey how do you find social media  wellness  here's the good news the good news is  that recognizing a problem is the first  step to fixing it so hearing this talk  is just that step one recognize the  problem do you know the power of  suggestion when someone tells you about  something and then you start seeing it  everywhere  that's why awareness is critical because  now you will at least be better able to  recognize these effects if and when they  happen to you  the second thing you're going to do is  audit your social media diet  the same way we monitor what goes into  our mouth monitor what goes into your  head and heart  ask yourself did that facebook scroll  make me feel better or worse off  how many times do i actually check likes  why am i responding this way to that  photo  and then ask yourself if you're happy  with the results and you might be and  that's okay  but if you're not move on to step three  create a better online experience  after my partner did his audit he  realized his self-worth was too tied up  in social media but particularly  celebrities reminding him of the things  he didn't have  so he unfollowed all brands and all  celebrities and that worked for him but  it might not be celebrities for you for  me i had to purge other people off my  timeline so let me tell you a secret you  do not have to follow your friends  the truth is that sometimes our friends  or the people we have on facebook as a  courtesy  they just suck online you find yourself  in the middle of this passive aggressive  status war you didn't even know is  happening or you're looking at 50 photos  of the same concert from the same angle  if you want to follow artists or  comedians or cats you can do that  the last thing you're going to do is  model good behavior  offline we're taught not to bully other  kids in the playground we're taught to  respect others and treat them how they  deserve to be treated we're taught not  to kick others when they're down or take  pleasure in their downfalls  social media is a tool  and it's a tool that can be used for  good for more positive groups for  revolutions for putting grumpy cat in  disney movies  tell you the internet is a weird place  so is social media hurting your mental  health the answer is it does not have to  social can tear you down yes  or it can lift you up where you leave  feeling better off or have an actual  laugh out loud  if i only have 24 hours in the day if  i'm gonna spend two of those hours on  social media then i prefer my  experiences to be full of inspiration  laughs motivation and a whole lot of  grumpy cat in disney movies happy number  nine have a morning routine with jay  shetty every weekday i wake up at 6 00  a.m i haven't set an alarm for a long  time but i know that my body will  naturally wake me up based on the  routine that i've created  i usually sleep for about eight hours a  night and i'm very grateful to have that  much time and i prioritize sleeping at  about 10 p.m every single day when i'm  up the first thing my mind wants to do  is say something like i feel so tired  i've got so much to do today  and i as soon as i wake up in that  moment rewire and reprogram it to say i  am happy i am healthy and i am healing  that simple internal affirmation and  statement rewires how i feel about what  i have that day i'm grateful for the  opportunities in the work i have i'm  healing with the challenges i have and  my body is healthy because i'm able to  get out of bed i don't look at my phone  in the morning trust me i don't always  follow this i've broken that rule many  many times i'm not perfect i've failed  at it countless times but i keep trying  to rise back to this rule why because i  feel amazing the amount of clarity in my  mind the stillness that i experience  when my phone isn't the first thing that  i see  is  inconceivable and i highly recommend  that you try it out just for seven days  don't look at your phone first thing in  the morning and see how different you  feel for the rest of the day i promise  you you're going to feel more energetic  more productive and more creative than  you've ever been before now i've been  practicing something for a long time now  called mindful brushing i brush each  tooth for around four seconds before i  move the toothbrush this mindful process  allows me to start the day at my own  time in my own pace so often before i  used to rush brushing my teeth  not only would i be damaging my gums and  damaging my teeth by the way dentists  will tell you about that i actually was  starting my day off in a panic with  stress  slowing down the first activity of the  day  allows you to start and move at your own  pace in your own time  the next thing i do after a shower is  meditate meditation has been a part of  my daily practice for the last 16 years  where i've meditated for at least two  hours a day often this is broken up  throughout the days on a busy day on an  amazing day i get to do two hours first  thing in the morning sometimes i'll do  one hour in the morning one hour in the  evening or sometimes i'll do 30 minute  slots four times a day this really  allows me to find that center to find  that alignment and find that balance  throughout the day my meditation  practice includes three types of  meditation  visualization and mantra for those of  you that have read think like a monk you  know that i break down these practices  inside the book so that you can practice  them at home as well breath work is a  phenomenal way of calming and centering  my body visualization is a great way to  prepare my mind for all the meetings and  events and everything i have to do  throughout the day  and the mantra is for my soul it's to  help me feel connected to my purpose  make me feel connected to divine energy  and the supreme source so that i can be  an instrument of that joy of that  happiness and of that love in the world  with everyone i mean recently i've  updated my morning meditation using the  aura ring app where i take a moment to  check my body status what this does is  that it helps me measure my heart rate  my breath rate and my skin temperature  just for a moment just to take a status  check just to check in with myself and  say how am i feeling how am i doing  right now it even asks me if i'm feeling  better or worse after the moment and  that allows me to get that really  critical piece of insight into how i  feel right now when you bump into  someone you always ask them how are you  doing how are you feeling  take a moment to ask yourself that with  the moments feature on the aura ring app  after my meditation practice i literally  take two to three minutes to set my  intention for the day and my attention  for the day  i found that this activity allows me to  create the energy and the mental space i  need to try and live by higher values  now again i fail at this all the time i  can go through the day and then realize  i haven't been loving i haven't been  kind i haven't been the nicest person  that day but the thing is when i write  it down in the morning i promise you i'm  more likely to be what i write on the  page i'm reminded to be what i write on  the page i also take a moment to write  three things i'm grateful for  and no matter how hard it is they're at  prom i promise you there are some days  where i'm like i've just got complaints  i've got no